From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 22:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] sidebar: a Lists view of thread lists, and Move to List
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

0048's store has held thread lists since story 031 -- named categories,
a thread in at most one list, deleting a list keeps its threads -- and
nothing in Zeo showed or wrote one. This patch is that interface, the
fourth consumer of the store after 0049, 0065 and 0068: story 038,
2026-10-20. Lists are a separate view, like History, and never rearrange
the thread list (operator decision 2026-10-09): tags are fine-grained,
lists are categories.

What it does. A "Thread Lists" button (IconName::ListTree, "Show/Hide
Thread Lists") sits in the sidebar footer next to the History clock and
toggles a third view, Lists, in place of the thread list; History and
Lists switch to each other directly, and the action
agents_sidebar::ToggleThreadLists does what the button does. Each list is a
collapsible group, in the store's order, whose header carries the list's
name and how many threads it shows, also while collapsed; its rows are
its threads, most recent activity first, each with its project's name,
and an archived thread or a draft is hidden but stays in the list. A
click on a row opens the thread where it lives -- this window, another
window holding its project, or this window with the project opened --
and keeps the Lists view shown; a right-click shows the thread list's own
context menu. That menu, everywhere a thread row has one, gains "Move to
List" after "Tags…" (0068): every list with the current one marked,
"Remove from List" while the thread is in one, and "New List…", which
opens the Lists view with a name field that creates the list and files
the thread. In the view, "New List" creates a list, and a header's
context menu renames, deletes (no confirmation: its threads lose only the
grouping), and moves it up or down. A name of 1-64 characters, unique
ignoring case, is required: a refused name keeps the field open with "A
list name must be 1 to 64 characters" or "A list with this name already
exists". Escape closes the field and changes nothing. The view is saved
with the window and restored after a restart; collapsed lists are
remembered per window, in memory, as the Pinned section's fold is.

How. crates/sidebar/src/lists_view.rs holds the row model, the view
switch and the key guard; lists_view/lists_render.rs draws it,
lists_view/move_to_list.rs is the submenu and lists_view/lists_manage.rs
the name field and the header menu. Rows are built in rebuild_contents,
while the view is shown, by one call of 0049's build_pinned_rows with
every expanded list's thread ids, split back per list by thread id: one
project label per project across lists and one live-state pass, so a row
resolves its workspace, agent icon and label exactly as a Pinned row
does, and is drawn and clicked through render_thread's own on_click
(activate_thread, or open_workspace_and_activate_thread for a project
open nowhere) -- not 0040's router, which is why this patch stays
ungrouped. The view's rows and its ListState live in
SidebarView::Lists(ListsViewState), so they are dropped with the view;
the ListState is spliced over the changed middle range rather than reset,
so a fold, a filing in another window or a reorder keeps the scroll. The
store observers Sidebar::new already detaches keep rebuilding the
entries in every view, which is what refreshes an open Lists view; the
thread list's `contents` never change, so leaving the view shows its
rows, order and folds as they were.

Limits, by design. The thread list's selection-based keyboard actions
(select next/previous/first/last, child/parent, confirm, fold, move,
archive, pin, rename) read an index of `contents`, which no Lists row
has: while the Lists view is shown and the sidebar root has focus they
are stopped in the capture phase and the selection is cleared, so
keyboard navigation between Lists rows is inert (out of scope), while a
name field or a context menu keeps its own keys. Lists rows offer no
drag: 0065's thread_row_drag returns the bare row while Lists is shown,
since its index would reorder an unrelated group. Every name field --
create, rename, create-and-file -- is drawn where the New List control
is, not over the renamed header. ToggleThreadLists has no default key
binding, so none is displaced; it is bindable and in the command palette.
A saved Lists view read by a build without this patch fails that build's
sidebar-state deserialization once and falls back to the defaults.

Failure: a store refusal changes nothing and logs one warning naming ids
only -- the thread by ThreadId::to_key_string(), the list by its ListId,
and the store's error, whose Display names ids only -- never a title or a
list name. Choosing a thread's current list changes and logs nothing.
Without the store (try_global is None) there is no button, no "Move to
List" and no Lists view: the action does nothing, and a window saved
with the view shown restores the thread list.

Upstream touch points. crates/sidebar/src/sidebar.rs only through 12
hunks and 41 added lines, none removed: the module lines; the
ToggleThreadLists action; SerializedSidebarView::Lists;
SidebarView::Lists(ListsViewState); the Lists hook in rebuild_contents,
before the unchanged notified_threads.retain, which builds the rows and
adds their ids to current_thread_ids so a filed thread keeps its
notification; move_to_list_submenu after 0068's tags_menu_entry; the
footer's .children(render_lists_button) after the History button;
toggle_archive's Lists arm (History from Lists); serialize's and
restore's Lists arms; the Render root's toggle_lists listener and
capture_lists_view_keys; and the Render arm drawing render_lists_view.
crates/sidebar/src/organization.rs: 4 lines, 0065's drag guard above.
No Cargo.toml, no Cargo.lock, no new crate.

0063 is regenerated on top with one change besides context and offsets:
it sets its new PinnedBuildContext::is_devtools in this patch's Lists
hook too, so a devtools thread filed in a list carries the same globe
badge as in its group. 0028-0030 are regenerated with no other change
than offsets and index lines.

Tests: 54, in seven files under crates/sidebar/src/sidebar_lists_tests/
-- rows (9), view (9), render (8), open (4), keyboard (4), move_to_list
(8), manage (12) -- with their shared fixtures in sidebar_lists_tests.rs
and support.rs.

UNGROUPED and unconditional, right after 0068: it consumes 0048's store
and 0049's build_pinned_rows, extends 0065's drag guard and puts its
menu entry after 0068's, and needs no adapter. The claude-agent-acp-plus
patches that also edit sidebar.rs (0028-0030) and 0063 are generated on
top of it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---

diff --git a/crates/sidebar/src/lists_view.rs b/crates/sidebar/src/lists_view.rs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1939a76
--- /dev/null
+++ b/crates/sidebar/src/lists_view.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,425 @@
+//! The Lists view of the agent sidebar: the user's thread lists, one group per list.
+
+use std::cmp::Reverse;
+use std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};
+use std::sync::Arc;
+
+use agent_ui::thread_metadata_store::{ThreadMetadata, ThreadMetadataStore};
+use agent_ui::thread_organization::{ListId, ThreadList, ThreadOrganizationStore};
+use agent_ui::{ArchiveSelectedThread, RenameSelectedThread, ThreadId};
+use editor::Editor;
+use editor::actions::ToggleFold;
+use gpui::{Action, Entity, Focusable, ListAlignment, ListState};
+use menu::{
+    Confirm, SelectChild, SelectFirst, SelectLast, SelectNext, SelectParent, SelectPrevious,
+};
+use ui::{Tooltip, prelude::*};
+use zed_actions::editor::{MoveDown, MoveUp};
+
+use crate::{Sidebar, SidebarView, ThreadEntry, TogglePinSelectedThread, ToggleThreadLists};
+
+mod lists_manage;
+mod lists_render;
+mod move_to_list;
+
+pub(crate) use move_to_list::move_to_list_submenu;
+
+/// One row of the Lists view. Thread rows carry ids only: the rebuild turns them into full
+/// thread rows, which need a window and an `App` this model deliberately does without.
+#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub(crate) enum ListsRow {
+    Header {
+        list_id: ListId,
+        name: SharedString,
+        /// The rows the list shows when expanded, kept while collapsed so the count never
+        /// depends on the fold (R2.1).
+        shown: usize,
+        collapsed: bool,
+    },
+    Thread {
+        list_id: ListId,
+        thread_id: ThreadId,
+    },
+    /// The "No threads" row of an expanded list with nothing to show (R2.6).
+    Empty { list_id: ListId },
+}
+
+/// The Lists view's rows: a header per list in `lists`' order, then, unless the list is in
+/// `collapsed`, its shown threads most recently active first, or `Empty` when it shows none.
+/// Members are only read, so a hidden one stays filed (R2.10).
+pub(crate) fn build_list_rows<'a>(
+    lists: &[ThreadList],
+    members_of: impl Fn(ListId) -> Vec<ThreadId>,
+    metadata_of: impl Fn(ThreadId) -> Option<&'a ThreadMetadata>,
+    collapsed: &HashSet<ListId>,
+) -> Vec<ListsRow> {
+    let mut rows = Vec::new();
+    for list in lists {
+        // The Pinned section's rule (`build_pinned_rows`), so a thread hidden there is hidden
+        // here too (R2.4).
+        let mut shown: Vec<&ThreadMetadata> = members_of(list.id)
+            .into_iter()
+            .filter_map(&metadata_of)
+            .filter(|metadata| !metadata.archived && !metadata.is_draft())
+            .collect();
+        // Recent mode's time (R2.3); the id breaks ties because the store hands members over in
+        // no meaningful order, and a row must not jump between rebuilds.
+        shown.sort_by_cached_key(|metadata| {
+            (
+                Reverse(Sidebar::thread_display_time(metadata)),
+                metadata.thread_id.to_key_string(),
+            )
+        });
+
+        let is_collapsed = collapsed.contains(&list.id);
+        rows.push(ListsRow::Header {
+            list_id: list.id,
+            name: list.name.clone(),
+            shown: shown.len(),
+            collapsed: is_collapsed,
+        });
+        if is_collapsed {
+            continue;
+        }
+        if shown.is_empty() {
+            rows.push(ListsRow::Empty { list_id: list.id });
+        }
+        rows.extend(shown.into_iter().map(|metadata| ListsRow::Thread {
+            list_id: list.id,
+            thread_id: metadata.thread_id,
+        }));
+    }
+    rows
+}
+
+/// One row of the Lists view as drawn: `ListsRow` with each thread turned into a full row.
+#[derive(Clone)]
+pub(crate) enum ListsEntry {
+    Header {
+        list_id: ListId,
+        name: SharedString,
+        shown: usize,
+        collapsed: bool,
+    },
+    Thread(Arc<ThreadEntry>),
+    Empty {
+        list_id: ListId,
+    },
+}
+
+impl ListsEntry {
+    /// Whether two rows are the same row, drawn at the same height, so the list need not be
+    /// re-measured: neither a header's name and count nor a thread's live state changes it.
+    fn is_same_row(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {
+        match (self, other) {
+            (Self::Header { list_id: a, .. }, Self::Header { list_id: b, .. })
+            | (Self::Empty { list_id: a }, Self::Empty { list_id: b }) => a == b,
+            (Self::Thread(a), Self::Thread(b)) => a.metadata.thread_id == b.metadata.thread_id,
+            _ => false,
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// The Lists view's rows as drawn: `build_list_rows`' model, its threads turned into full rows by
+/// `thread_rows`. Empty without the store, since there are no lists.
+pub(crate) fn build_lists_contents(
+    collapsed: &HashSet<ListId>,
+    thread_rows: impl FnOnce(&[ThreadId]) -> Vec<Arc<ThreadEntry>>,
+    cx: &App,
+) -> Vec<ListsEntry> {
+    let Some(store) = ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx) else {
+        return Vec::new();
+    };
+    let store = store.read(cx);
+    let metadata = ThreadMetadataStore::global(cx).read(cx);
+    let rows = build_list_rows(
+        store.lists(),
+        |list| store.threads_in_list(list),
+        |thread_id| metadata.entry(thread_id),
+        collapsed,
+    );
+
+    // One call for every list, so a project is labelled alike in all of them and the live state
+    // is gathered once per rebuild. A thread is in one list at most, so its id finds its row.
+    let thread_ids: Vec<ThreadId> = rows
+        .iter()
+        .filter_map(|row| match row {
+            ListsRow::Thread { thread_id, .. } => Some(*thread_id),
+            ListsRow::Header { .. } | ListsRow::Empty { .. } => None,
+        })
+        .collect();
+    let mut rows_by_id: HashMap<ThreadId, Arc<ThreadEntry>> = thread_rows(&thread_ids)
+        .into_iter()
+        .map(|row| (row.metadata.thread_id, row))
+        .collect();
+    rows.into_iter()
+        .filter_map(|row| match row {
+            ListsRow::Header {
+                list_id,
+                name,
+                shown,
+                collapsed,
+            } => Some(ListsEntry::Header {
+                list_id,
+                name,
+                shown,
+                collapsed,
+            }),
+            ListsRow::Thread { thread_id, .. } => {
+                rows_by_id.remove(&thread_id).map(ListsEntry::Thread)
+            }
+            ListsRow::Empty { list_id } => Some(ListsEntry::Empty { list_id }),
+        })
+        .collect()
+}
+
+/// The threads the Lists view shows, so the rebuild keeps their notifications.
+pub(crate) fn lists_thread_ids(entries: &[ListsEntry]) -> impl Iterator<Item = ThreadId> + '_ {
+    entries.iter().filter_map(|entry| match entry {
+        ListsEntry::Thread(row) => Some(row.metadata.thread_id),
+        ListsEntry::Header { .. } | ListsEntry::Empty { .. } => None,
+    })
+}
+
+/// The Lists view's per-window state. It is dropped when the view is left, so a window's folds
+/// last only while the view is shown, and no hidden view keeps rows to rebuild.
+pub(crate) struct ListsViewState {
+    pub(crate) collapsed: HashSet<ListId>,
+    /// Built by `rebuild_contents` while the view is shown; the indexes `render_thread` gets.
+    pub(crate) lists_entries: Vec<ListsEntry>,
+    lists_list_state: ListState,
+    /// The view's one name field, while open. It lives here so leaving the view drops it.
+    pub(crate) name_field: Option<ListsNameField>,
+}
+
+/// What confirming the Lists view's name field is for.
+#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub(crate) enum NameFieldMode {
+    /// The "New List" control: the list is created after the existing ones (R5.1).
+    Create,
+    /// A header menu's "Rename List…" (R5.4).
+    Rename(ListId),
+    /// Opened by a thread menu's "New List…": the new list is created and that thread filed in
+    /// it (R4.5, R4.6).
+    CreateAndFile(ThreadId),
+}
+
+/// The open name field: a single-line editor, the mode its confirm acts in, and the store's last
+/// refusal of a name, shown under it (R5.2, R5.3).
+pub(crate) struct ListsNameField {
+    pub(crate) mode: NameFieldMode,
+    pub(crate) editor: Entity<Editor>,
+    pub(crate) message: Option<SharedString>,
+}
+
+impl Default for ListsViewState {
+    fn default() -> Self {
+        Self {
+            collapsed: HashSet::default(),
+            lists_entries: Vec::new(),
+            // The thread list's own settings, so the two views scroll alike.
+            lists_list_state: ListState::new(0, ListAlignment::Top, px(1000.)),
+            name_field: None,
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+// `SidebarView` is `Debug`, but neither a `ListState` nor a thread row is.
+impl std::fmt::Debug for ListsViewState {
+    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {
+        f.debug_struct("ListsViewState")
+            .field("collapsed", &self.collapsed)
+            .field("lists_entries", &self.lists_entries.len())
+            .field(
+                "name_field",
+                &self.name_field.as_ref().map(|field| field.mode),
+            )
+            .finish_non_exhaustive()
+    }
+}
+
+impl Sidebar {
+    /// The footer's "Thread Lists" button; none without the store, since there are no lists to
+    /// show (R1.8).
+    pub(crate) fn render_lists_button(&self, cx: &mut Context<Self>) -> Option<IconButton> {
+        ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx)?;
+        let is_lists = matches!(self.view, SidebarView::Lists(_));
+        Some(
+            IconButton::new("thread-lists", IconName::ListTree)
+                .icon_size(IconSize::Small)
+                .toggle_state(is_lists)
+                .tooltip(move |_, cx| {
+                    let label = if is_lists {
+                        "Hide Thread Lists"
+                    } else {
+                        "Show Thread Lists"
+                    };
+                    Tooltip::for_action(label, &ToggleThreadLists, cx)
+                })
+                .on_click(cx.listener(|this, _, window, cx| {
+                    this.toggle_lists(&ToggleThreadLists, window, cx);
+                })),
+        )
+    }
+
+    /// The button's click and the action share this, so the two cannot drift apart (R1.9).
+    pub(crate) fn toggle_lists(
+        &mut self,
+        _: &ToggleThreadLists,
+        window: &mut Window,
+        cx: &mut Context<Self>,
+    ) {
+        match &self.view {
+            SidebarView::Lists(_) => self.leave_lists(window, cx),
+            SidebarView::ThreadList | SidebarView::Archive(_) => self.show_lists(window, cx),
+        }
+    }
+
+    pub(crate) fn show_lists(&mut self, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
+        // The action has no button to hide behind: without the store there is nothing to show.
+        if ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx).is_none() {
+            return;
+        }
+        // Only History's subscription lives here; the store observers from `Sidebar::new` are
+        // detached, so they keep rebuilding the entries in every view.
+        self._subscriptions.clear();
+        self.view = SidebarView::Lists(ListsViewState::default());
+        // The thread list's hovered index would name a different row of this view.
+        self.hovered_thread_index = None;
+        // History's filter editor, which may have held focus, has just been dropped.
+        self.focus_handle.focus(window, cx);
+        self.update_entries(cx);
+        self.serialize(cx);
+        cx.notify();
+    }
+
+    /// Back to the thread list, rebuilt as it was: its rows, order and folds never left
+    /// `contents` (R1.7). Only a selection and a hover are dropped (`drop_lists_indexes`).
+    pub(crate) fn leave_lists(&mut self, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
+        self.drop_lists_indexes();
+        self.show_thread_list(window, cx);
+    }
+
+    /// History from the Lists view, leaving no Lists index behind for the thread list.
+    pub(crate) fn show_history_from_lists(&mut self, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
+        self.drop_lists_indexes();
+        self.show_archive(window, cx);
+    }
+
+    /// A Lists row sets the selection (by renaming) and the hover with an index of the Lists
+    /// view's rows, which names another row of the thread list.
+    fn drop_lists_indexes(&mut self) {
+        self.selection = None;
+        self.hovered_thread_index = None;
+    }
+
+    /// The thread list's keyboard handlers read `selection` as an index of `contents`, which no
+    /// Lists row has: while this view is shown they would act on a row nobody can see. They are
+    /// stopped on the sidebar's root, in the capture phase, and only while the root itself has
+    /// focus, so a rename field or a context menu still gets its own keys.
+    pub(crate) fn capture_lists_view_keys<E: InteractiveElement>(root: E, cx: &Context<Self>) -> E {
+        root.capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<SelectNext>(cx))
+            .capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<SelectPrevious>(cx))
+            .capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<SelectFirst>(cx))
+            .capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<SelectLast>(cx))
+            .capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<SelectChild>(cx))
+            .capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<SelectParent>(cx))
+            .capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<MoveDown>(cx))
+            .capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<MoveUp>(cx))
+            .capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<Confirm>(cx))
+            .capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<ToggleFold>(cx))
+            .capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<ArchiveSelectedThread>(cx))
+            .capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<TogglePinSelectedThread>(cx))
+            .capture_action(Self::lists_key_guard::<RenameSelectedThread>(cx))
+    }
+
+    fn lists_key_guard<A: Action>(
+        cx: &Context<Self>,
+    ) -> impl Fn(&A, &mut Window, &mut App) + 'static {
+        cx.listener(|this, _: &A, window, cx| {
+            if !matches!(this.view, SidebarView::Lists(_)) {
+                return;
+            }
+            this.selection = None;
+            if this.focus_handle.is_focused(window) {
+                cx.stop_propagation();
+            }
+        })
+    }
+
+    /// A saved Lists view is reopened only where the store exists; elsewhere the sidebar stays on
+    /// the thread list (R1.10).
+    pub(crate) fn restore_lists_view(&mut self, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
+        if ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx).is_some() {
+            cx.defer_in(window, |this, window, cx| this.show_lists(window, cx));
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Keeps the rows `rebuild_contents` built, re-measuring only the changed middle range, as
+    /// `apply_list_state_diff` does for the thread list: the scroll position and the unchanged
+    /// rows' measurements survive a fold, a filing in another window and a reorder.
+    pub(crate) fn set_lists_entries(&mut self, entries: Vec<ListsEntry>) {
+        let SidebarView::Lists(state) = &mut self.view else {
+            return;
+        };
+        let old = &state.lists_entries;
+        let prefix_len = old
+            .iter()
+            .zip(&entries)
+            .take_while(|(old_row, new_row)| old_row.is_same_row(new_row))
+            .count();
+        let suffix_len = old[prefix_len..]
+            .iter()
+            .rev()
+            .zip(entries[prefix_len..].iter().rev())
+            .take_while(|(old_row, new_row)| old_row.is_same_row(new_row))
+            .count();
+        let old_changed = prefix_len..old.len() - suffix_len;
+        let new_changed_count = entries.len() - prefix_len - suffix_len;
+        state.lists_entries = entries;
+        if !old_changed.is_empty() || new_changed_count > 0 {
+            state
+                .lists_list_state
+                .splice(old_changed, new_changed_count);
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Opens the view's name field in `mode`, replacing any field already open (one at a time),
+    /// and gives it focus, since it is typed into. Does nothing outside the Lists view.
+    pub(crate) fn open_lists_name_field(
+        &mut self,
+        mode: NameFieldMode,
+        window: &mut Window,
+        cx: &mut Context<Self>,
+    ) {
+        if !matches!(self.view, SidebarView::Lists(_)) {
+            return;
+        }
+        let editor = cx.new(|cx| {
+            let mut editor = Editor::single_line(window, cx);
+            editor.set_placeholder_text("List name", window, cx);
+            editor
+        });
+        editor.focus_handle(cx).focus(window, cx);
+        if let SidebarView::Lists(state) = &mut self.view {
+            state.name_field = Some(ListsNameField {
+                mode,
+                editor,
+                message: None,
+            });
+        }
+        cx.notify();
+    }
+
+    /// A header click folds the list in this window only (R2.5): the fold lives in the view's
+    /// own state, never in the store.
+    fn toggle_list_collapsed(&mut self, list_id: ListId, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
+        let SidebarView::Lists(state) = &mut self.view else {
+            return;
+        };
+        if !state.collapsed.remove(&list_id) {
+            state.collapsed.insert(list_id);
+        }
+        self.update_entries(cx);
+    }
+}
diff --git a/crates/sidebar/src/lists_view/lists_manage.rs b/crates/sidebar/src/lists_view/lists_manage.rs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..dea0f3f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/crates/sidebar/src/lists_view/lists_manage.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,277 @@
+//! Managing lists from the Lists view: its name field (New List, New List…, Rename List…) and a
+//! header's menu (Rename List…, Delete List, Move Up, Move Down). Every rule a name must meet is
+//! the store's: the field only maps the store's refusals to text, so the two cannot disagree.
+
+use agent_ui::thread_organization::{ListId, OrganizeError, ThreadOrganizationStore};
+use menu::Confirm;
+use ui::{ContextMenu, prelude::*, right_click_menu};
+
+use super::{ListsNameField, NameFieldMode};
+use crate::{Sidebar, SidebarView};
+
+// R5.2 and R5.3's wording. They are shown under the field and never logged: a name is the
+// user's text, and a refusal of it is an answer to the user, not a fault.
+const LIST_NAME_LENGTH_MESSAGE: &str = "A list name must be 1 to 64 characters";
+const LIST_NAME_DUPLICATE_MESSAGE: &str = "A list with this name already exists";
+
+impl Sidebar {
+    /// The "New List" control's click: the name field, open to create a list (R2.7, R2.11).
+    pub(crate) fn start_new_list(&mut self, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
+        self.open_lists_name_field(NameFieldMode::Create, window, cx);
+    }
+
+    /// "Rename List…": the name field, prefilled with the list's name and all of it selected, so
+    /// typing replaces it (R5.4).
+    fn start_rename_list(&mut self, list_id: ListId, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
+        let Some(name) = ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx).and_then(|store| {
+            store
+                .read(cx)
+                .lists()
+                .iter()
+                .find(|list| list.id == list_id)
+                .map(|list| list.name.clone())
+        }) else {
+            // Deleted in another window after this menu opened.
+            let error = OrganizeError::UnknownList { list_id };
+            log::warn!("sidebar: rename refused for list {list_id}: {error}");
+            return;
+        };
+        self.open_lists_name_field(NameFieldMode::Rename(list_id), window, cx);
+        if let Some(field) = self.lists_name_field() {
+            field.editor.update(cx, |editor, cx| {
+                editor.set_text(name, window, cx);
+                editor.select_all(&editor::actions::SelectAll, window, cx);
+            });
+        }
+    }
+
+    fn lists_name_field(&self) -> Option<&ListsNameField> {
+        match &self.view {
+            SidebarView::Lists(state) => state.name_field.as_ref(),
+            SidebarView::ThreadList | SidebarView::Archive(_) => None,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Closes the field with nothing applied, and gives focus back to the sidebar, which the
+    /// field took when it opened.
+    fn close_lists_name_field(&mut self, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
+        if let SidebarView::Lists(state) = &mut self.view
+            && state.name_field.take().is_some()
+        {
+            self.focus_handle.focus(window, cx);
+            cx.notify();
+        }
+    }
+
+    fn show_lists_name_message(&mut self, message: &'static str, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
+        if let SidebarView::Lists(state) = &mut self.view
+            && let Some(field) = &mut state.name_field
+        {
+            field.message = Some(message.into());
+            cx.notify();
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Enter in the field: the store creates or renames, and its answer decides whether the field
+    /// closes (R4.9, R5.1–R5.4, R5.9). Runs from the field's key handler, outside any store update.
+    fn confirm_lists_name_field(&mut self, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
+        let Some(field) = self.lists_name_field() else {
+            return;
+        };
+        let mode = field.mode;
+        let name = field.editor.read(cx).text(cx);
+        let Some(store) = ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx) else {
+            return;
+        };
+        let result = match mode {
+            NameFieldMode::Create | NameFieldMode::CreateAndFile(_) => store
+                .update(cx, |store, cx| store.create_list(&name, cx))
+                .map(Some),
+            NameFieldMode::Rename(list_id) => store
+                .update(cx, |store, cx| store.rename_list(list_id, &name, cx))
+                .map(|()| None),
+        };
+        match result {
+            Ok(created) => {
+                self.close_lists_name_field(window, cx);
+                // A refused filing keeps the new list and warns, as the submenu's own does.
+                if let (NameFieldMode::CreateAndFile(thread_id), Some(list_id)) = (mode, created) {
+                    self.move_thread_to_list(thread_id, Some(list_id), cx);
+                }
+            }
+            Err(OrganizeError::InvalidListName) => {
+                self.show_lists_name_message(LIST_NAME_LENGTH_MESSAGE, cx);
+            }
+            Err(OrganizeError::DuplicateListName { .. }) => {
+                self.show_lists_name_message(LIST_NAME_DUPLICATE_MESSAGE, cx);
+            }
+            // The list is gone (deleted in another window): there is nothing left to rename, so
+            // the field closes. The error names ids only, never the typed name (R5.9).
+            Err(error) => {
+                match mode {
+                    NameFieldMode::Rename(list_id) => {
+                        log::warn!("sidebar: rename refused for list {list_id}: {error}")
+                    }
+                    NameFieldMode::Create | NameFieldMode::CreateAndFile(_) => {
+                        log::warn!("sidebar: new list refused: {error}")
+                    }
+                }
+                self.close_lists_name_field(window, cx);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// "Delete List": no confirmation (story assumption). The store leaves each member in no list
+    /// with everything else it had (R5.5).
+    fn delete_list_from_header(
+        &mut self,
+        list_id: ListId,
+        window: &mut Window,
+        cx: &mut Context<Self>,
+    ) {
+        let Some(store) = ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx) else {
+            return;
+        };
+        if let Err(error) = store.update(cx, |store, cx| store.delete_list(list_id, cx)) {
+            log::warn!("sidebar: delete refused for list {list_id}: {error}");
+            return;
+        }
+        // The fold is per window and names a list that no longer exists.
+        if let SidebarView::Lists(state) = &mut self.view {
+            state.collapsed.remove(&list_id);
+        }
+        // A rename of the deleted list could only be refused on Enter.
+        if self
+            .lists_name_field()
+            .is_some_and(|field| field.mode == NameFieldMode::Rename(list_id))
+        {
+            self.close_lists_name_field(window, cx);
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// "Move Up"/"Move Down": one place from where the list is when clicked, which another window
+    /// may have changed since the menu opened (R5.6).
+    fn move_list_one_place(&mut self, list_id: ListId, up: bool, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
+        let Some(store) = ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx) else {
+            return;
+        };
+        let Some(index) = store
+            .read(cx)
+            .lists()
+            .iter()
+            .position(|list| list.id == list_id)
+        else {
+            let error = OrganizeError::UnknownList { list_id };
+            log::warn!("sidebar: move refused for list {list_id}: {error}");
+            return;
+        };
+        let to_index = if up {
+            index.saturating_sub(1)
+        } else {
+            index + 1
+        };
+        if let Err(error) = store.update(cx, |store, cx| store.move_list(list_id, to_index, cx)) {
+            log::warn!("sidebar: move refused for list {list_id}: {error}");
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// The open field, with its own Enter and Escape: it holds focus, so the Lists view's key
+    /// guards (which act only while the sidebar root has focus) never see these keys.
+    pub(super) fn render_lists_name_field(
+        &self,
+        field: &ListsNameField,
+        cx: &mut Context<Self>,
+    ) -> AnyElement {
+        v_flex()
+            .debug_selector(|| "LISTS_NAME_FIELD".into())
+            .w_full()
+            .px_1()
+            .gap_0p5()
+            // A single-line editor binds Enter to `Confirm`; `Newline` is captured too, as the
+            // thread rename field does, so no keymap turns Enter into a second line.
+            .capture_action(
+                cx.listener(|this, _: &editor::actions::Newline, window, cx| {
+                    this.confirm_lists_name_field(window, cx);
+                }),
+            )
+            .on_action(cx.listener(|this, _: &Confirm, window, cx| {
+                this.confirm_lists_name_field(window, cx);
+            }))
+            .on_action(
+                cx.listener(|this, _: &editor::actions::Cancel, window, cx| {
+                    this.close_lists_name_field(window, cx);
+                }),
+            )
+            .child(field.editor.clone())
+            .when_some(field.message.clone(), |this, message| {
+                let selector = message.clone();
+                this.child(
+                    div()
+                        .debug_selector(move || format!("LISTS_NAME_MESSAGE-{selector}"))
+                        .child(
+                            Label::new(message)
+                                .size(LabelSize::Small)
+                                .color(Color::Error),
+                        ),
+                )
+            })
+            .into_any_element()
+    }
+
+    /// A list header with its right-click menu. Which entries it offers is read when the menu
+    /// opens, so Move Up and Move Down match the order at that moment (R5.7).
+    pub(super) fn render_lists_header_menu(
+        &self,
+        list_id: ListId,
+        header: AnyElement,
+        cx: &mut Context<Self>,
+    ) -> AnyElement {
+        let sidebar = cx.weak_entity();
+        right_click_menu(SharedString::from(format!("lists-header-menu-{list_id}")))
+            .trigger(move |_, _, _| header)
+            .menu(move |window, cx| {
+                let position = ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx).and_then(|store| {
+                    let lists = store.read(cx).lists();
+                    lists
+                        .iter()
+                        .position(|list| list.id == list_id)
+                        .map(|index| (index, lists.len()))
+                });
+                let can_move_up = position.is_some_and(|(index, _)| index > 0);
+                let can_move_down = position.is_some_and(|(index, count)| index + 1 < count);
+                let sidebar = sidebar.clone();
+                ContextMenu::build(window, cx, move |menu, _window, _cx| {
+                    let act =
+                        |action: fn(&mut Sidebar, ListId, &mut Window, &mut Context<Sidebar>)| {
+                            let sidebar = sidebar.clone();
+                            move |window: &mut Window, cx: &mut App| {
+                                sidebar
+                                    .update(cx, |sidebar, cx| action(sidebar, list_id, window, cx))
+                                    .ok();
+                            }
+                        };
+                    menu.entry("Rename List…", None, act(Sidebar::start_rename_list))
+                        .entry("Delete List", None, act(Sidebar::delete_list_from_header))
+                        .when(can_move_up, |menu| {
+                            menu.entry(
+                                "Move Up",
+                                None,
+                                act(|sidebar, list_id, _, cx| {
+                                    sidebar.move_list_one_place(list_id, true, cx)
+                                }),
+                            )
+                        })
+                        .when(can_move_down, |menu| {
+                            menu.entry(
+                                "Move Down",
+                                None,
+                                act(|sidebar, list_id, _, cx| {
+                                    sidebar.move_list_one_place(list_id, false, cx)
+                                }),
+                            )
+                        })
+                })
+            })
+            .into_any_element()
+    }
+}
diff --git a/crates/sidebar/src/lists_view/lists_render.rs b/crates/sidebar/src/lists_view/lists_render.rs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1a7519d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/crates/sidebar/src/lists_view/lists_render.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,215 @@
+//! Drawing the Lists view: its headers, thread rows, "No threads" rows and empty state. The rows
+//! themselves are built by `rebuild_contents` (see `build_lists_contents`); this only reads them.
+
+use std::sync::Arc;
+
+use agent_ui::thread_organization::ListId;
+use gpui::list;
+use ui::{ScrollAxes, Scrollbars, Tab, WithScrollbar, prelude::*};
+
+use super::ListsEntry;
+use crate::{ListEntry, Sidebar, SidebarView, ThreadEntry};
+
+impl Sidebar {
+    pub(crate) fn render_lists_view(
+        &mut self,
+        window: &mut Window,
+        cx: &mut Context<Self>,
+    ) -> impl IntoElement {
+        // The view selects no row, and a rename here sets `selection` to a Lists index that
+        // the thread list's handlers would read as one of theirs (see `capture_lists_view_keys`).
+        self.selection = None;
+        let SidebarView::Lists(state) = &self.view else {
+            return div().into_any_element();
+        };
+        // Every list has a header row, so no row means no list (R2.7).
+        if state.lists_entries.is_empty() {
+            return self.render_lists_empty_state(cx).into_any_element();
+        }
+        let list_state = state.lists_list_state.clone();
+        v_flex()
+            .flex_1()
+            .overflow_hidden()
+            // Above the lists, so a new list never needs a list to exist first (R2.11).
+            .child(
+                h_flex()
+                    .px_1()
+                    .py_1()
+                    .child(self.render_new_list_control(cx)),
+            )
+            .child(
+                v_flex()
+                    .relative()
+                    .flex_1()
+                    .overflow_hidden()
+                    .child(
+                        list(list_state.clone(), cx.processor(Self::render_lists_entry))
+                            .flex_1()
+                            .size_full(),
+                    )
+                    .custom_scrollbars(
+                        Scrollbars::new(ScrollAxes::Vertical).tracked_scroll_handle(&list_state),
+                        window,
+                        cx,
+                    ),
+            )
+            .into_any_element()
+    }
+
+    fn render_lists_entry(
+        &mut self,
+        ix: usize,
+        _window: &mut Window,
+        cx: &mut Context<Self>,
+    ) -> AnyElement {
+        let SidebarView::Lists(state) = &self.view else {
+            return div().into_any_element();
+        };
+        let Some(entry) = state.lists_entries.get(ix).cloned() else {
+            return div().into_any_element();
+        };
+        match entry {
+            ListsEntry::Header {
+                list_id,
+                name,
+                shown,
+                collapsed,
+            } => self.render_lists_header(ix, list_id, name, shown, collapsed, cx),
+            ListsEntry::Thread(thread) => self.render_lists_thread(ix, &thread, cx),
+            ListsEntry::Empty { list_id } => {
+                let name = list_name_above(&state.lists_entries[..ix], list_id);
+                h_flex()
+                    .debug_selector(move || format!("LISTS_NO_THREADS-{name}"))
+                    .h(Tab::content_height(cx))
+                    .w_full()
+                    .pl_4()
+                    .child(
+                        Label::new("No threads")
+                            .size(LabelSize::Small)
+                            .color(Color::Muted),
+                    )
+                    .into_any_element()
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    fn render_lists_header(
+        &self,
+        ix: usize,
+        list_id: ListId,
+        name: SharedString,
+        shown: usize,
+        collapsed: bool,
+        cx: &mut Context<Self>,
+    ) -> AnyElement {
+        let disclosure_icon = if collapsed {
+            IconName::ChevronRight
+        } else {
+            IconName::ChevronDown
+        };
+        let selector_name = name.clone();
+        let header = h_flex()
+            .id(("lists-header", ix))
+            .debug_selector(move || format!("LISTS_HEADER-{selector_name} ({shown})"))
+            .h(Tab::content_height(cx))
+            .w_full()
+            .pl_2()
+            .pr_1p5()
+            .gap_1()
+            .child(Label::new(name).color(Color::Muted).truncate())
+            // Shown while collapsed too, so a folded list still says how much it holds (R2.1).
+            .child(
+                Label::new(shown.to_string())
+                    .size(LabelSize::Small)
+                    .color(Color::Muted),
+            )
+            .child(
+                Icon::new(disclosure_icon)
+                    .size(IconSize::Small)
+                    .color(Color::Muted),
+            )
+            .cursor_pointer()
+            .on_click(cx.listener(move |this, _: &gpui::ClickEvent, _window, cx| {
+                this.toggle_list_collapsed(list_id, cx);
+            }))
+            // No `block_mouse_except_scroll`, unlike the Pinned header (whose sticky copy is drawn
+            // over the rows): it would hide the header from its own right-click menu's hitbox.
+            .into_any_element();
+        self.render_lists_header_menu(list_id, header, cx)
+    }
+
+    /// The thread list's own row, so its click, menu and buttons behave as they do there; `ix`
+    /// indexes the Lists view's rows, which the row's hover and menu ids are keyed by.
+    fn render_lists_thread(
+        &self,
+        ix: usize,
+        thread: &Arc<ThreadEntry>,
+        cx: &mut Context<Self>,
+    ) -> AnyElement {
+        let is_active = self
+            .active_entry
+            .as_ref()
+            .is_some_and(|active| active.matches_entry(&ListEntry::Thread(thread.clone())));
+        let title = thread.metadata.display_title();
+        let project = thread.pinned_project_label.clone().unwrap_or_default();
+        // Keyboard selection is not offered in this view, so no row draws the focus border.
+        let row = self.render_thread(ix, thread, is_active, false, cx);
+        div()
+            .debug_selector(move || format!("LISTS_ROW-{title} · {project}"))
+            .child(row)
+            .into_any_element()
+    }
+
+    /// R2.7's empty state: what lists are for, and the way to make one.
+    fn render_lists_empty_state(&self, cx: &mut Context<Self>) -> impl IntoElement {
+        v_flex()
+            .debug_selector(|| "LISTS_EMPTY_STATE".into())
+            .flex_1()
+            .p_4()
+            .gap_2()
+            .items_center()
+            .justify_center()
+            .child(Label::new("No lists yet").color(Color::Muted))
+            .child(
+                Label::new("Lists group threads by category.")
+                    .size(LabelSize::Small)
+                    .color(Color::Muted),
+            )
+            .child(self.render_new_list_control(cx))
+    }
+
+    /// The "New List" control, or in its place the name field while one is open, so the field
+    /// shows both in the empty state and above the lists.
+    fn render_new_list_control(&self, cx: &mut Context<Self>) -> AnyElement {
+        if let SidebarView::Lists(state) = &self.view
+            && let Some(field) = &state.name_field
+        {
+            return self.render_lists_name_field(field, cx);
+        }
+        div()
+            .debug_selector(|| "LISTS_NEW_LIST".into())
+            .child(
+                Button::new("lists-new-list", "New List")
+                    .start_icon(Icon::new(IconName::Plus).size(IconSize::Small))
+                    .label_size(LabelSize::Small)
+                    .on_click(cx.listener(|this, _, window, cx| this.start_new_list(window, cx))),
+            )
+            .into_any_element()
+    }
+}
+
+/// The name of the list whose header is the nearest one above a row.
+fn list_name_above(entries: &[ListsEntry], list_id: ListId) -> SharedString {
+    entries
+        .iter()
+        .rev()
+        .find_map(|entry| match entry {
+            ListsEntry::Header {
+                list_id: header_id,
+                name,
+                ..
+            } if *header_id == list_id => Some(name.clone()),
+            _ => None,
+        })
+        .unwrap_or_default()
+}
diff --git a/crates/sidebar/src/lists_view/move_to_list.rs b/crates/sidebar/src/lists_view/move_to_list.rs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6cf6d09
--- /dev/null
+++ b/crates/sidebar/src/lists_view/move_to_list.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,112 @@
+//! The "Move to List" submenu of a thread's context menu, and the filing it does.
+
+use agent_ui::ThreadId;
+use agent_ui::thread_organization::{ListId, ThreadOrganizationStore};
+use gpui::WeakEntity;
+use ui::{ContextMenu, prelude::*};
+
+use super::NameFieldMode;
+use crate::{Sidebar, SidebarView};
+
+/// Adds the "Move to List" submenu to a thread's menu (R4.1); nothing without the store, which
+/// is where lists live. The sidebar is held weakly, as every entry of that menu holds it.
+pub(crate) fn move_to_list_submenu(
+    menu: ContextMenu,
+    sidebar: WeakEntity<Sidebar>,
+    thread_id: ThreadId,
+    cx: &App,
+) -> ContextMenu {
+    if ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx).is_none() {
+        return menu;
+    }
+    // The builder runs each time the submenu opens, so it reads the lists as they are then, not
+    // as they were when the thread menu opened.
+    menu.submenu("Move to List", move |mut submenu, _window, cx| {
+        let Some(store) = ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx) else {
+            return submenu;
+        };
+        let store = store.read(cx);
+        let current = store
+            .organization(thread_id)
+            .and_then(|organization| organization.list_id);
+        let file_in = |list: Option<ListId>| {
+            let sidebar = sidebar.clone();
+            move |_window: &mut Window, cx: &mut App| {
+                sidebar
+                    .update(cx, |sidebar, cx| {
+                        sidebar.move_thread_to_list(thread_id, list, cx);
+                    })
+                    .ok();
+            }
+        };
+        let lists = store.lists();
+        for list in lists {
+            submenu = submenu.toggleable_entry(
+                list.name.clone(),
+                current == Some(list.id),
+                IconPosition::Start,
+                None,
+                file_in(Some(list.id)),
+            );
+        }
+        if current.is_some() {
+            submenu = submenu.entry("Remove from List", None, file_in(None));
+        }
+        if !lists.is_empty() {
+            submenu = submenu.separator();
+        }
+        let sidebar = sidebar.clone();
+        submenu.entry("New List…", None, move |window, cx| {
+            sidebar
+                .update(cx, |sidebar, cx| {
+                    sidebar.new_list_for_thread(thread_id, window, cx);
+                })
+                .ok();
+        })
+    })
+}
+
+impl Sidebar {
+    /// Files `thread_id` in `list`, or in no list for `None` (R4.2, R4.8). Called from UI
+    /// handlers only: a store write inside a `ThreadMetadataStore` update would re-enter it.
+    pub(crate) fn move_thread_to_list(
+        &mut self,
+        thread_id: ThreadId,
+        list: Option<ListId>,
+        cx: &mut Context<Self>,
+    ) {
+        let Some(store) = ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx) else {
+            return;
+        };
+        let current = store
+            .read(cx)
+            .organization(thread_id)
+            .and_then(|organization| organization.list_id);
+        // The current list is the marked entry; choosing it must not act as a toggle (R4.3).
+        if current == list {
+            return;
+        }
+        let result = store.update(cx, |store, cx| store.set_list(thread_id, list, cx));
+        // The store's errors name ids only; a title or a list name never reaches a log (R4.7).
+        if let Err(error) = result {
+            log::warn!(
+                "sidebar: list change refused for thread {}: {error}",
+                thread_id.to_key_string()
+            );
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// "New List…": the Lists view, with its name field open to file this thread in the list it
+    /// creates (R4.5). A view already shown keeps its folds.
+    fn new_list_for_thread(
+        &mut self,
+        thread_id: ThreadId,
+        window: &mut Window,
+        cx: &mut Context<Self>,
+    ) {
+        if !matches!(self.view, SidebarView::Lists(_)) {
+            self.show_lists(window, cx);
+        }
+        self.open_lists_name_field(NameFieldMode::CreateAndFile(thread_id), window, cx);
+    }
+}
diff --git a/crates/sidebar/src/organization.rs b/crates/sidebar/src/organization.rs
index 55c7c53..e7e0d95 100644
--- a/crates/sidebar/src/organization.rs
+++ b/crates/sidebar/src/organization.rs
@@ -410,6 +410,10 @@ impl Sidebar {
         if ThreadOrganizationStore::try_global(cx).is_none() || self.has_filter_query(cx) {
             return row;
         }
+        // A Lists row's `ix` indexes that view's rows, not `contents`: no group order to drag in.
+        if matches!(self.view, crate::SidebarView::Lists(_)) {
+            return row;
+        }
         let Some(origin) = row_origin(&self.contents.entries, ix) else {
             return row;
         };
